---
/**
 * `/privacy` — the privacy page's route (AAP 0.4.2, 0.8.4).
 *
 * Three regions and nothing else, in this order: the page top, then the body the owner supplies.
 * The design reference has no frame for this page — it is one of the three frameless routes that
 * use the base layout with the About page's top block — so the top block follows that block's
 * composition and nothing below it imitates a template section (rule W-1, AAP 0.2.1, 0.8.4).
 *
 * Copy discipline. Every word on the page arrives as an expression over a content module:
 * `privacy.meta` for the document metadata, `privacy.heading` for the page's single `h1`,
 * `privacy.body` for the text beneath it, and `global.wordmark` for the company name the top block
 * opens with. No string is typed here, and neither is an `alt`, `title`, `aria-label` or
 * `placeholder` value, so the content layer stays the one source of user-visible text (rules W-1,
 * B-4; AAP 0.4.3, 0.7.2). While A7's text is outstanding, `privacy.ts` holds the project's
 * placeholder marker, which `scripts/launch-check.ts` refuses to let a live build publish (AAP
 * 0.9.5, WT-10); supplying the owner's copy means editing that module and leaves this page as it
 * stands.
 *
 * Nothing is invented to fill the page. There is no effective date, no company number, no
 * registered address, no jurisdiction and no contact clause — each would be a claim the
 * specification does not make, which is what rule B-4 and AAP 0.7.6 forbid. The route is complete
 * and the one field still open is the owner's own.
 *
 * The document head, the skip link, the header, the `<main>` landmark and the footer belong to the
 * base layout, so this file owns only what renders inside the slot. It reads no configuration,
 * opens no request handler, ships no script, holds no image element and reaches no host but this
 * one (rules B-1, B-2, W-2).
 */
import { global } from '../content/global.ts';
import { privacy } from '../content/privacy.ts';

import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

/**
 * The body region's geometry: the page's content column, then the band rhythm the site's sections
 * share — `space-60` above and below at the narrow width and `space-80` at the wide one, which is
 * the vertical padding the reference gives this page's bands and the bottom of its page top at
 * each width (AAP 0.8.4). The inset and the column width come from `.content`, so the 1440 and 390
 * artboards have one source (PA-1), and the wide step reads the theme's breakpoint token through
 * Tailwind's `wide:` variant, so the 900 step moves as one line (PA-5).
 */
const BODY_LAYOUT = 'content pt-60 pb-60 wide:pt-80 wide:pb-80';

/**
 * The body copy: the design's prose step, `type-body-3` — Urbanist 400 at leading 1.5, 16 at the
 * wide width and 10 at the narrow one — justified, which is the alignment the reference declares on
 * its own prose style rather than a choice made here, and set in the page's primary ink so its
 * 17.08:1 contrast on the Cream ground never depends on the block a composer places it in (AAP
 * 0.2.2, 0.6.3; rule B-3).
 *
 * The measure is what makes the justification read. The reference constrains prose to a narrow
 * column at the wide width and lets it fill the column at the narrow one, so the paragraph holds
 * the whole column below the breakpoint and a third of it above. That third is 448 wide against the
 * 16-pixel prose step — twenty-eight times the type size, and about fifty-six characters a line,
 * which sits between the sixteen to twenty times the reference's own prose columns measure and the
 * length a justified setting stays comfortable at. The half-column the page top's lead paragraph
 * takes would run to roughly eighty-four characters here, because the body step is half the lead's
 * size.
 */
const BODY = 'type-body-3 text-ink-chocolate text-justify wide:max-w-1/3';
---

<BaseLayout meta={privacy.meta} nav={global.nav} footer={global.footer}>
  {/*
    The page top, in the About page's composition: the company name opens the block, the
    bracket-framed frame holds the authored `top` panel, and the page's single `h1` is A7's
    heading (AAP 0.2.1, 0.2.4, 0.8.4; rule B-3).

    The wordmark is passed here because the reference draws a right-aligned display wordmark in
    this slot at both of its widths, and `Wordmark` renders a string only from its `text` prop, so
    the words have to be handed in from the content layer rather than typed (AAP 0.7.2). A7 gives
    this page no eyebrow, no lead paragraph, no status label and no action, so the block opens on
    the wordmark, the heading and the panel and carries none of those members — and none is
    invented in their place (rule B-4).
  */}
  <Hero
    heading={privacy.heading}
    illustration="top"
    wordmark={true}
    wordmarkText={global.wordmark}
  />

  {/*
    The body region: one block whose only content is the owner's copy, taken whole from the content
    layer and rendered as ordinary text rather than markup, so no sanitising step is needed and no
    case, truncation or sentence split alters what the specification will say.

    No heading stands above it and none may be added. The content module carries exactly one
    heading, which the page top has already rendered as the page's `h1`; a second heading here
    would either repeat that string or introduce one the specification does not supply, and
    inventing copy is what rule B-4 forbids. That is why this route renders no `SectionHeading`:
    the component has nothing of A7's to show until a distinct section heading exists in the
    content module.
  */}
  <section class={BODY_LAYOUT}>
    <p class={BODY}>{privacy.body}</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
